WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.800
Textbooks that cost students hundreds of dollars each term could be duplicated at near zero marginal cost once written, yet the price stays high because of how courses are chosen and paid for. This is First Principles Daily, Episode 66. A single required textbook bundle for an undergraduate course often runs between 150 and 300 dollars. Sometimes the total climbs higher when the bundle includes access codes that lock the material to one term.

00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:50.940
Once the words exist, sending another copy to a student costs essentially nothing beyond server space or a few cents of ink and paper. The difference between those two numbers is not explained by the physical materials inside the book. It comes from a set of institutional habits that treat a repeatable good like a custom built machine each time.

00:00:51.940 --> 00:01:03.820
The gap becomes visible the moment a department keeps the same core text across multiple terms, yet the per student charge does not drop after the first cohort has paid for the initial authoring and review.

00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:17.720
Publishers still follow a model built around physical printing runs and annual revision cycles. A new addition appears every two or three years, often with only modest changes to problem sets or examples.

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:29.260
Those modest changes render the previous addition obsolete for many assignments. Professors select the text, yet the cost falls on students who have no direct voice in the choice.

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:44.140
This separation removes the usual market pressure that would otherwise reward lower prices. Access codes add another layer because they convert a durable object into a time limited license that expires at the end of the term.

00:01:44.140 --> 00:01:59.780
The license blocks resale and forces each new student to start fresh. Campus bookstores and third party platforms capture margins on both new and used copies, but the underlying list price remains anchored by the publisher's list.

00:02:00.620 --> 00:02:07.480
Faculty often default to the same publisher they used in graduate school or the one whose representative visited most recently.

00:02:08.480 --> 00:02:13.860
Evaluating alternatives requires time that is not compensated so the familiar choice wins.

00:02:14.700 --> 00:02:25.280
Departments rarely maintain a central fund to buy perpetual licenses or to commission replacements. The result is a market in which the buyer and the payer are different people.

00:02:26.140 --> 00:02:32.960
New additions arrive on a predictable schedule and the marginal cost of distribution has fallen while the price has not.

00:02:32.960 --> 00:02:43.820
These patterns feel normal inside the industry because every participant optimized for their own constraints rather than for the lowest sustainable cost of delivering the content.

00:02:44.380 --> 00:02:51.940
When a professor considers switching, the immediate personal cost is the hours needed to rewrite lecture notes and realign homework.

00:02:52.680 --> 00:02:56.500
The savings accrue to students who are not in the room during the decision.

00:02:56.500 --> 00:03:06.500
Over time, this produces a stable equilibrium in which list prices stay elevated even as digital delivery removes most printing and shipping expenses.

00:03:07.240 --> 00:03:13.220
The same equilibrium explains why used book markets shrink each time and access code is required.

00:03:13.940 --> 00:03:21.340
The code itself is priced to cover the full list amount rather than the near zero cost of regenerating a new key on a server.

00:03:21.340 --> 00:03:30.300
If the words and diagrams already exist, the raw cost of delivering them is the price of storing a few megabytes and transmitting them once.

00:03:31.160 --> 00:03:38.640
That figure sits close to zero for digital distribution and only a few dollars even for a printed copy on commodity paper.

00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:42.280
A typical new textbook sells for roughly $200.

00:03:43.280 --> 00:03:54.360
Dividing the finished price by the material and distribution floor produces an idiot index in the range of 50 to several hundred, depending on whether the copy is digital or physical.

00:03:55.180 --> 00:03:57.740
The gap does not sit in the paper or the ink.

00:03:58.340 --> 00:04:03.580
It accumulates in the repeated authoring and reviewing cycles that produce each new addition.

00:04:03.580 --> 00:04:16.060
It also accumulates in the sales force that visits departments, in the platform fees for access capital code systems, and in the carrying costs of unsold inventory that must be written down.

00:04:16.720 --> 00:04:21.140
Marketing budgets sized for a national textbook adoption further widen the spread.

00:04:21.880 --> 00:04:31.480
The largest single addition comes from the decision to treat each course section as a separate sale, rather than as one more instance of a good already paid for.

00:04:31.480 --> 00:04:39.820
When a department continues using the same text for several years, the per student cost should fall sharply after the first cohort.

00:04:40.460 --> 00:04:43.220
Yet the list price rarely reflects that reality.

00:04:43.880 --> 00:04:51.780
The institutional rules around addition churn and access expiration keep the effective price from tracking the physical or digital floor.

00:04:52.780 --> 00:05:05.280
Every extra dollar above the cost of storage and transmission is therefore traceable to choices about who pays, how often the content is declared obsolete, and whether the license survives beyond a single term.

00:05:06.020 --> 00:05:12.980
One can see the arithmetic clearly by imagining a department of 200 students using the same text for four years.

00:05:12.980 --> 00:05:17.500
The first year cohort pays the full authoring and marketing overhead.

00:05:18.240 --> 00:05:22.840
Subsequent cohorts should in principle pay only the marginal delivery cost.

00:05:23.460 --> 00:05:29.960
Yet the published price stays fixed because each new student is still required to purchase a fresh access code.

00:05:30.620 --> 00:05:42.440
The objection that content must still be created and reviewed is valid, but that cost is incurred once and then spread across thousands of students rather than re-encurd with every new term.

00:05:42.440 --> 00:05:51.240
An open educational resource funded once by a consortium of universities could remove the recurring authoring cost for core subjects.

00:05:52.140 --> 00:06:02.800
Faculty would need incentives, such as teaching release time or departmental credit to adopt and adapt those resources instead of defaulting to the familiar commercial text.

00:06:03.560 --> 00:06:08.740
Institutions could buy perpetual licenses or underwrite the maintenance of a shared platform.

00:06:08.740 --> 00:06:14.260
That step would convert a recurring student expense into a one-time capital cost.

00:06:14.580 --> 00:06:23.900
The first concrete move would be to identify high enrollment courses where existing open texts already cover the required topics at acceptable quality.

00:06:24.580 --> 00:06:33.800
The second would be to create a small fund that compensates faculty for the work of aligning those texts with local learning objectives and updating problem sets.

00:06:34.800 --> 00:06:44.500
The third would be to negotiate with accreditation bodies so that departments receive credit for measured learning outcomes rather than for the brand of the textbook they assigned.

00:06:45.200 --> 00:06:51.040
Hard limits remain because original authorship and peer review still require compensated time.

00:06:51.700 --> 00:06:58.420
Some advanced or rapidly changing fields may need frequent updates that cannot be supplied entirely by volunteers.

00:06:59.420 --> 00:07:04.760
Quality control and version management also demand ongoing labor that cannot be wished away.

00:07:05.240 --> 00:07:17.180
Still, the arithmetic shows that once the initial investment is made, the marginal cost per additional student drops close to the cost of bandwidth, not the cost of a new printing and distribution cycle.

00:07:17.180 --> 00:07:32.180
A department that spends $50,000 once to adapt and maintain an open text for a course serving 800 students per year would, after three years, have spent less per student than the cumulative list price payments under the current model.

00:07:32.620 --> 00:07:36.600
The remaining obstacles are therefore not technical but organizational.

00:07:37.600 --> 00:07:49.240
They concern who writes the check for the initial adaptation, who receives credit for the labor and how the institution measures success when the metric shifts from textbook brand to measured student cost.

00:07:49.760 --> 00:07:58.700
When the person choosing a product never sees its price tag, the price can remain far above the cost of simply repeating what already exists.

00:07:59.700 --> 00:08:12.600
When a good can be copied at negligible cost, any price that stays high year after year is being held there by rules about ownership, revision cycles, and who bears the expense rather than by any physical constraint.

00:08:13.240 --> 00:08:22.820
Tomorrow, the show returns with another concrete case or another domain where the same gap between finished price and raw material floor is waiting to be examined.

00:08:23.820 --> 00:08:32.760
The first signal that changes underway will be a department publishing its own measured cost per student for a course that previously relied on commercial bundles.

